FDM patch 0004: limits on the wheel (BOGEY) strut model, so that a tipped-over, inverted or buried
aircraft cannot be pushed with unbounded force (session 05)

What is wrong in JSBSim (FGLGear.cpp, unchanged since long before v1.3.3)
  For a wheel, JSBSim treats the leg as a telescoping strut. With p = LGearProj, the cosine of the
  angle between the leg and the ground normal:
    compression       = depth of the wheel point below the ground / p
    compression speed = normal speed of the contact point / p
    normal force      = strut force (spring x compression + damper x speed) / p
  Nothing limits any of the three. As the aircraft tips over, p goes to zero and the force grows like
  1/p^2; an aircraft that has sunk into the ground (the 737 model has no airframe contact points)
  reports compressions of tens to millions of feet. REPORT-04 found forces of 5 million times the
  weight; REVIEW-codex-04 showed that limiting the compression alone makes things worse, because the
  force is still divided by p.

What this patch does, when it is switched on
  1. Projection floor. p is not allowed below min-projection in any of the three formulas. Up to that
     tilt the leg's formula is JSBSim's; beyond it the wheel keeps pushing as it did at that tilt, like
     a solid point of the airframe. (p <= 0, the leg pointing away from the ground, gives no force,
     as in JSBSim.)
  2. Travel. If the compression the geometry asks for exceeds max-travel-leg-fraction times the height
     of the centre of gravity above the wheel (both from the aircraft's own file: the wheel's location
     and the CG), the strut has failed or the airframe is in the ground: the wheel gives no force and
     reports no contact. This is what stops an aircraft that sank deep from being thrown when a wheel
     "finds" the ground again.
  3. Force cap. One strut cannot push with more than max-force-weights times the aircraft's weight.
  STRUCTURE (airframe) contact points are not changed.

Properties (all under gear/strut-limits/)
    enabled                    0 = JSBSim unchanged (default), 1 = the limits act. Other values are refused.
    min-projection             default 0.5 (a tilt of 60 deg; the force is then at most 4 times the
                               upright formula's). Must be above 0 and at most 1.
    max-force-weights          default 5. The test aircraft's own dampers ask for up to 3.7 weights per
                               wheel in a landing at their limit sink rate (f16, c310); real gear fails
                               well below 5. Must be positive.
    max-travel-leg-fraction    default 0.5: 1.9 ft (c310) to 2.9 ft (f16) for the test aircraft; their
                               hardest landings within limits compress 0.7 ft. Must be positive.
    projection-floored, force-capped, travel-exceeded
                               read-only counters (wheel-steps in which each limit acted); the
                               simulation never reads them.
  A refused setting throws and keeps its old value. Like JSBSim's integrator selections (and patch
  0003's settings), they return to their defaults when the model is re-initialised.

Where the defaults come from
  They were fixed before the patch was built or run, from the aircraft files and from the landings of
  the fixtures on JSBSim's default integrators (GATES-05.md): large enough that no landing within the
  aircraft's limits touches them (checked: the counters stay at zero in every G2d landing, and those
  runs are bit-identical with the patch on and off; REPORT-05), small enough to bound the force.

What it does not do
  It bounds the force of the wheels; it does not make a crash realistic. An aircraft without airframe
  contact points still sinks into the ground once its wheels are gone, and airframe points keep
  JSBSim's own (unlimited, but geometrically sound) spring and damper. Stopping the simulation at that
  point is the wrapper's job (crash containment).

How it is switched off
  With enabled = 0 (the default) every statement computes what unpatched JSBSim computes. Checked:
  bit-identical logs (tests/native/verify_key.sh patched unpatched, tests/native/verify_wasm_patch_off.sh).

